        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">On a manual treadmill you are the motor.</strong> On a flat
            folding walker, start on the steepest slope with the lowest resistance, lean from the
            ankles and push the belt back with short steps, hands resting on the rails. On a curved
            trainer, walk up the front of the curve to start, move forward to speed up and drift back
            to slow down.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Stop by easing off rather than braking, build up over a fortnight rather than a day, and
            treat sharp or lingering pain as a reason to stop. If you are still choosing a machine,
            our guide to the
            <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best manual treadmills for walking and running</a>
